{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfdSFBDyJ3XEibPP4Wxqc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the candidate, and the self-check prints `OK`. It is likely to fit far worse than the current best model.\n\n**Name:** `bayesian_generator_personal_suspicion`\n\n**Hypothesis:** People judge randomness the normative way. For each sequence they work out the chance that a fair coin made it rather than a non-random source: a biased coin, a coin that tends to repeat or to switch, or a sequence built to be symmetric (a palindrome, or one whose second half is the first half reversed with heads and tails swapped). They pick the sequence that is more likely to be random. The one distortion is a personal prior suspicion: each person has their own starting belief that a sequence was made by a non-random source.\n- A suspicious person rates most sequences as almost certainly non-random, so they only tell apart the most random-looking ones.\n- A trusting person rates most sequences as almost certainly random, so they only tell apart the most regular-looking ones.\n\nSo people differ in how consistently they agree with the majority, depending on which pairs they see. That is the critique's one finding that survived the false-discovery correction (people's agreement with the majority varies more than the best model predicts).\n\n**Model:** For each sequence, `compute_features` returns the log Bayes factor of fair coin versus the four non-random sources, each with equal prior. There are no other features. Free parameters:\n- each person's suspicion, drawn from a population distribution (non-centred);\n- one shared sensitivity to the difference between the two sequences' chances of being random;\n- each person's guessing rate.\n\nThe model sizes per-person parameters at 400 slots, so the next design can predict new participants.\n\n**Self-check results** (a short smoke fit: 1 chain, 100 draws):\n- **Fit quality:** ELPD-LOO is −4931, versus −3470.5 for the current best model. That is roughly 1,400–1,500 nats worse, so if admitted it will probably be pruned at the end of the experiment. The pure Bayesian models tried before also lost by wide margins.\n- **Reliability warning:** the check flagged PSIS-LOO as unreliable, with 5.6% of trials having Pareto k > 0.5 against a 1% tolerance. That alone did not stop the check from passing.\n- **Not checked:** convergence (a single chain cannot give R-hat) and novelty against the other models. Admission runs both. I expect the novelty gate to pass because the mechanism is very different from every model in the set, but I haven't confirmed it.\n\nFiles are in `/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run1/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/iter_2/candidate_1/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3310,"cache_read_input_tokens":85131,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3310,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7609d47a-7f38-4aec-9e98-7ed935a1f34f","uuid":"e491967c-bcd1-4c7d-a7b4-f96f016e75a6","timestamp":"2026-10-02T12:48:04.660Z","request_id":"req_011CfdSFA9j9dsKcR6fCtikK"}
